hadiths[22]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1864","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: Allah, Glorified and Exalted be He, has ninety-nine names. Whoever memorizes them (that is, believes in their meanings and acts accordingly) will enter Paradise. And Allah Almighty is Odd and loves the odd number (that is why He did not mention a complete hundred names, although Allah has countless names)."
"1865","Sayyiduna Farwah bin Nawfal Ashja'i (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her), ""What supplication did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to make to Allah?"" She said that he (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: ""O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the evil of the deeds I have done and from the evil of the deeds I have not done."
"1866","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: ""O Allah! I have submitted to You, and I have believed in You, and I have put my trust in You, and I have turned to You, and with Your help I fight the enemies. O Master! I seek refuge in Your honor from being led astray by You, and there is no deity worthy of worship except You, and You are the Ever-Living who does not die, while jinn and mankind die."
"1867","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey and morning came, he would say: The one who hears has heard the praise of Allah and His good trial. O our Lord! Remain with us (that is, help us) and bestow Your grace upon us, and I seek refuge in You from the Hellfire."
"1868","´Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari رضي الله عنه narrates from the Noble Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم that` he صلى الله عليه وسلم used to supplicate: O Allah! Forgive my mistakes, my ignorance, and my excesses in my affairs, and forgive whatever You know better than I do. O Allah! Forgive my intentional sins and my unintentional sins, my joking and my errors and my deliberate actions, and all of these are from me. O Master! Forgive my past and future sins, my hidden and apparent sins, and those which You know better than I do. You are the One Who brings forward and the One Who delays, and You have power over everything."
"1869","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: O Allah! Set right my religion for me, which is the safeguard and protector of my Hereafter, and set right my worldly affairs for me, in which is my livelihood and life. And set right my Hereafter for me, to which is my return. And make my life an increase for me in every good, and make my death a relief for me from every evil. (This supplication is comprehensive for every purpose.)"
"1870","`Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that` he (peace be upon him) used to say: O Allah! I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity (from the unlawful), and richness of the heart."
"1871","Sayyiduna Zaid bin Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I will say to you only what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah! I seek refuge with You from incapacity, laziness, cowardice, miserliness, old age, and the torment of the grave. O Allah! Grant my soul piety and purify it, for You are the best to purify it. You are its Guardian and Master. O Allah! I seek refuge with You from knowledge that does not benefit, from a heart that does not humble itself before You, from a soul that is never satisfied, and from a supplication that is not answered."
"1872","Sayyiduna Abu Malik Ashja'i narrates from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah! When I ask from my Lord, what should I say?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""Say: O Allah! Forgive my sins, have mercy on me, protect me (from sins), and grant me (lawful and pure) sustenance."" And while saying these words, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fold one finger at a time, until all were folded except the thumb. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""These words will combine for you the benefits of both this world and the Hereafter."
"1873","'Abdul Aziz (Ibn Suhaib) says that Qatadah asked Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Which supplication did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) make most frequently?"" Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said that he (peace and blessings be upon him) would most often supplicate: ""O Allah, grant us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and save us from the punishment of Hell."" And Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), whenever he wanted to supplicate, would make this supplication, and whenever he made any other supplication, he would include this supplication in it."
"1874","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Say this, ""O Allah! Guide me and make me upright."" And he said that when asking for this supplication, keep in mind that guidance means guidance to the path, and uprightness (being straight) means the straightness of an arrow."
"1875","‘Abdullah bin ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: Three men were traveling when a severe rain overtook them, so they took shelter in a cave of a mountain. Meanwhile, a rock fell from the mountain and blocked the mouth of the cave. They said to one another: Think of your righteous deeds which you have done for Allah’s sake, and supplicate to Him by means of those deeds; perhaps Allah will remove this rock for you. So one of them said: My parents were old and weak, and I had a wife and small children. I used to graze sheep and goats for them. When I would return in the evening after grazing, I would milk the animals and begin with my parents, giving them to drink before my children. One day, a tree took me far away (i.e., I found pasture very far), so I did not return home until evening, and I found my parents asleep. I milked as usual and stood by their heads with the milk. I disliked to wake them from their sleep, and I disliked to give my children before them. The children were crying at my feet out of hunger. So my and their condition remained like this until morning (i.e., I stood all night with the milk waiting for them to wake, and the children kept crying; neither did I drink nor did I give to the children). O Allah! If You know that I did this for Your pleasure, then open a way through this rock so that we may see the sky. So Allah opened it a little, and they saw the sky through it. The second said: O Allah! The matter is that I had a cousin whom I loved as a man loves a woman (i.e., I was extremely in love with her). I inclined towards her and desired her (i.e., intended to commit fornication). She refused and said: I will not agree unless you give me one hundred dinars. I strove and collected one hundred dinars and brought them to her. When I sat with her for the sexual act (i.e., with the intention of intercourse), she said: O servant of Allah! Fear Allah and do not break the seal unlawfully (i.e., do not take my virginity without marriage). So I got up from her. O Allah! If You know that I did this for Your pleasure, then open the cave a little more. Allah opened it a little more (i.e., the opening became larger). The third said: O Allah! I hired a man for a faraq (a vessel that holds sixteen ratl of grain) of rice as wages. When he finished his work, he said: Give me my due. I placed the faraq of rice before him, but he did not take it. I sowed those grains (and there was blessing in it), until I gathered from that wealth cows, oxen, and their shepherds. Then that laborer came to me and said: Fear Allah and do not withhold my right. I said: Go and take the cows, oxen, and their shepherds. He said: Fear Allah (the Compeller) and do not mock me. I said: I am not mocking; take the cows, oxen, and the shepherds. So he took them. O Allah! If You know that I did this for Your pleasure, then open what remains. So Allah opened the remaining mouth of the cave (and they came out of the cave)."
"1876","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite this supplication at times of hardship (and difficulty): ""There is no true deity except Allah, the Great, the Forbearing. There is no true deity except Allah, the Lord of the Great Throne. There is no true deity except Allah, the Lord of the heavens, the Lord of the earth, and the Lord of the Noble Throne."
"1877","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: The servant’s supplication is always accepted, as long as he does not supplicate for a sin or for severing ties of kinship, and as long as he does not hasten. The people asked, “O Messenger of Allah! What does it mean to hasten?” He (peace be upon him) said: That he says, “I have supplicated and supplicated, but I do not think it will be accepted,” then he becomes disheartened and abandons supplication. (This displeases the Master, and then He does not accept it. The servant should always have hope in the grace and mercy of his Master, and if the supplication is not accepted in this world, its reward will be given in the Hereafter.)"
"1878","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should say, ""O Allah! If You will, forgive me. O Allah! If You will, have mercy on me."" Rather, he should be firm in supplication, for Allah does whatever He wills; there is none to compel Him."
"1879","`Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` I heard the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""There is a moment during the night in which, if any Muslim asks Allah Almighty for goodness of this world or the Hereafter, Allah grants it to him. And this (moment) occurs every night."
"1880","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Our Lord, Blessed and Exalted is He, descends to the lowest heaven every night in the last third of the night and says: Who is there who supplicates to Me, so that I may answer his supplication? Who is there who asks of Me, so that I may give to him? Who is there who seeks forgiveness from Me, so that I may forgive him?"
"1881","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Noble Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: When you hear the crowing of a rooster, ask Allah for His bounty, for the rooster sees an angel. And when you hear the braying of a donkey, seek refuge with Allah from Satan (say: ""A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajim""), for it sees a devil."
"1882","Safwan (and he was Ibn Abdullah bin Safwan, and Umm al-Darda was in his marriage) said: I came to (the land of) Sham and went to the house of Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him). But I did not find him, and I met Lady Umm al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with her). She said to me: Are you intending to perform Hajj this year? I said: Yes. Umm al-Darda said: Then pray for me, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say that a Muslim's supplication for his brother in his absence is accepted. There is an appointed angel at his head; when he prays for his brother's good, the angel says: Ameen, and the same be for you. Then I went out to the market and met Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), and he also narrated the same from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."
"1883","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited a Muslim who had become as weak as a chick due to illness (i.e., he had become very weak and frail). The Prophet (peace be upon him) asked him, ""Did you used to supplicate or ask Allah for something?"" He replied, ""Yes! I used to say: O Allah! Whatever punishment You are going to inflict upon me in the Hereafter, inflict it upon me in this world."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said, ""Glory be to Allah! How can you bear Allah's punishment in this world? Why did you not say: O Allah! Grant me goodness in this world and goodness in the Hereafter, and save me from the punishment of Hellfire."" Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) prayed to Allah the Exalted for him, and Allah Almighty cured him."
"1884","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: None of you should wish for death because of any calamity or affliction that befalls him. If he must wish, then he should say: O Allah! Keep me alive as long as life is better for me, and cause me to die when death is better for me."
"1885","`Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that` the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: None of you should wish for death, nor should he pray for death before it comes to him. For when any one of you dies, his deeds come to an end, and for the believer, the longer he lives, the more good he does (because he performs more righteous deeds)."
